        q: "Why is customer churn prediction valuable to a bank?",
        a: "<span class=\"star-label\">Situation</span> Banks spend significant resources acquiring new customers — estimates put the cost of acquisition at 5–25× the cost of retaining an existing one.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Task</span> Understand the business case for building a churn prediction model and how it changes the bank's ability to intervene.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Action</span> Churn prediction lets the bank identify at-risk customers before they leave and intervene proactively — offering targeted incentives or outreach to those most likely to churn. Without a model, the bank must either run expensive blanket retention campaigns across all customers or react only after someone has already left. A model directs retention resources to where they are most needed.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Result</span> A correctly calibrated churn model maximizes retention ROI: the bank intervenes only where the probability of loss justifies the cost of the intervention, rather than spending uniformly on every customer regardless of churn risk."
