        q: "The dataset had ~20% churn. Why is that a problem for machine learning?",
        a: "<span class=\"star-label\">Situation</span> Only 20% of customers in the dataset had churned — the remaining 80% had not. This creates a significant class imbalance between the minority (churned) and majority (retained) classes.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Task</span> Understand why class imbalance causes standard ML models to fail and what must be done to address it.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Action</span> With only 20% positive examples, a naive model can achieve 80% accuracy by predicting 'no churn' for every single customer — without learning anything about what actually causes churn. Most algorithms optimize for overall accuracy, so the minority class gets systematically under-predicted. The model appears impressive on paper but misses almost every actual churner, which is precisely what the business cares about most.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Result</span> This is the class imbalance problem: a metric that looks good (80% accuracy) masks a model that is completely useless for its intended purpose. It must be addressed with specialized techniques — not ignored."
