        q: "What is edge AI and what role does the NVIDIA Jetson Nano play?",
        a: "<span class=\"star-label\">Situation</span> The autonomous robots needed to process RGB-D camera data and run the SpotFi localization algorithm in real time during a live competition match — latency constraints ruled out cloud-based inference.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Task</span> Explain the concept of edge AI and describe why the NVIDIA Jetson Nano was the right compute platform for this application.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Action</span> Edge AI means running AI inference directly on the device — at the 'edge' of the network — rather than sending data to a cloud server. For real-time autonomous systems, a cloud round-trip would add 200ms+ of latency per decision cycle — unacceptable during a live match. The NVIDIA Jetson Nano is a compact, energy-efficient processor designed specifically for edge AI inference: it runs neural networks and computer vision algorithms locally, fast enough for real-time robotics without a network connection. In the P2P design refinement, each robot's Jetson Nano also handled the distributed data processing load — receiving observations from peer robots via V5 Radio and fusing them with its own sensor data, all within real-time match constraints.<br><br><span class=\"star-label\">Result</span> Sub-millisecond local inference with no network dependency — the right performance profile for autonomous competition robotics. The Jetson Nano's role expanded from single-robot processing to participating in a distributed four-robot compute mesh."
